m method triangle-free process happy ending problem\n\n## Summary\n\nIn this plenary lecture at ICM 2026, Robert Morris presents a comprehensive overview of Ramsey theory, focusing on recent results and open problems. He begins with the historical origins, including Frank Ramsey’s theorem and the ‘happy ending problem’ posed by Esther Klein, which led to the rediscovery of Ramsey’s theorem. Morris then defines Ramsey numbers and discusses classical bounds, such as the simple upper bound of 4^k and Erdős’s probabilistic lower bound of 2^{k/2}. He introduces the asymmetric version, highlighting the case of R(3,k), where he details the evolution of bounds from Erdős’s geometric construction to the semi-random method and the triangle-free process, culminating in the exact asymptotic constant. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLower Bounds for Maximum Weight Bisections of Weighted Triangle-free Subcubic Graphs - Royal Holloway Research Portal (https://pure.royalholloway.ac.uk/en/publications/lower-bounds-for-maximum-weight-bisections-of-weighted-triangle-f/)\nciteturn3search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last month; Gerke, S, Gutin, G, Yeo, A & Zhou, Y 2026, 'Lower Bounds for Maximum Weight Bisections of Weighted Triangle-free Subcubic Graphs', Journal of Graph Theory. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.70056\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDescribing edges in triangle-free 3-polytopes | Sciact - Система учета научной деятельности ИМ СО РАН (https://sciact.math.nsc.ru/public/article/7289)\nciteturn3search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Данные  | Год: 2026, Том: 350, Номер: 2, Номер статьи : 115428, Страниц : DOI: 10.1016/j.disc.2026.115428 ... The purpose of our paper is to prove the following description of edges in triangle-free 3polytopes, where all parameters are best possible: “(3,3) × (4,8),(3,3) × (5,6),(3,4) × (4,5),(3,5) × (4,4)”.\n-------